**Short answer:**  
You can keep the 8 000 trail segments as a **GeoJSON source** and pair it with a **vector-tile basemap** (terrain + roads). 8 k features are well inside the “sweet-spot” for GeoJSON (< 2 MB, < 5 000 features) and the extra 3 000-ish features will still load quickly on desktop and most mobiles.  Only if the file grows past ~5 MB (or you start seeing > 3 s parse delays) would you consider turning the trails into vector tiles.

Below is a step-by-step guide that shows:

1. **Choosing the sources** – why GeoJSON works for the trails and why a vector basemap is the right choice for terrain/roads.  
2. **Wiring the style** – adding a hosted vector basemap, inserting the GeoJSON source, and ordering the layers so your trails sit **under** the basemap’s label layers (so road/place names stay readable).  
3. **Interactivity** – a click handler that shows the trail name and distance.  
4. **Optional “future-proof” tips** – when you might switch to vector tiles, how to generate them with `tippecanoe`, and how to keep the two-source pattern.

---

## 1️⃣ Source selection rationale

| Data | Typical size | Interaction needed? | Recommended source |
|------|--------------|---------------------|--------------------|
| **8 000 trail segments** (line geometries, a few properties) | Usually < 2 MB (even with a few hundred-meter-precision coordinates) | Click → popup with name & distance | **`geojson`** (inline or URL) |
| **Terrain + roads basemap** | Global or regional tiles, many zoom levels | No custom styling per feature, just background | **`vector`** (hosted style URL or TileJSON) |
| **Why not raster?** | Raster imagery (satellite, DEM) is larger and you lose crisp labels. | You need terrain *and* crisp road/label text → vector tiles give you both. |
| **Why not vector-tile trails now?** | You’d have to run a tile-generation pipeline (tippecanoe/Planetiler) and host the tiles. | 8 k features are small enough that the one-time GeoJSON download is cheaper and simpler. |

> **Rule of thumb from the skill:**  
> *< 2 MB / < 5 000 features → instant load.*  
> *5–20 MB / up to ~50 k features → 1–3 s parse, still usable on desktop.*  
> *> 50 MB / > 100 k features → risk of freeze; switch to vector tiles.*  

Your trail file is comfortably in the first two rows, so GeoJSON is fine.

---

## 2️⃣ Wiring the style (code)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – trails + terrain basemap</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map with a vector-tile basemap style.
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    // You can use any public vector style that includes terrain & roads.
    // Example: MapTiler's "basic" style with terrain (requires an API key)
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets-v2/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',
    center: [-122.45, 37.75], // adjust to your area
    zoom: 10
  });

  // 2️⃣ Add the trail GeoJSON source once the style is loaded.
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // If you have the file on the same domain you can just point to it:
    map.addSource('trails', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: '/data/trails.geojson',   // <-- your 8 k-segment file
      // optional: simplify on the fly (helps a little on very dense lines)
      // generateId: true   // gives each feature a stable id for queries
    });

    // 3️⃣ Add a line layer for the trails.
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'trails-line',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'trails',
      layout: {
        // Put the trails *under* the basemap's label layers.
        // We'll insert the layer just before the first symbol layer.
        // (see helper function below)
      },
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#ff7f00',
        'line-width': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['zoom'],
          10, 2,
          14, 4,
          18, 8
        ],
        'line-opacity': 0.9
      }
    });

    // 4️⃣ Insert the trail layer before the first symbol layer (labels)
    const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
    if (firstSymbolId) {
      map.moveLayer('trails-line', firstSymbolId);
    }

    // 5️⃣ Click handler – show name & distance in a popup.
    map.on('click', 'trails-line', (e) => {
      const props = e.features[0].properties;
      const name = props.name || 'Unnamed trail';
      const distance = props.distance ? `${props.distance} km` : '—';
      new maplibregl.Popup()
        .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
        .setHTML(`<strong>${name}</strong><br/>Distance: ${distance}`)
        .addTo(map);
    });

    // Change cursor on hover (nice UX)
    map.on('mouseenter', 'trails-line', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
    map.on('mouseleave', 'trails-line', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the snippet does

| Step | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **Basemap style** | A hosted vector style already bundles terrain (DEM) and road layers, plus the required `glyphs` and `sprite` assets. |
| **GeoJSON source** | One HTTP GET of the whole file; MapLibre parses it client-side. |
| **Line layer** | Renders the trails. The `line-width` interpolation makes them look good at all zooms. |
| **Layer ordering** | `moveLayer` puts the trails **under** the first symbol layer, so road/place labels stay on top. |
| **Popup** | `e.features[0].properties` gives you direct access to the GeoJSON attributes (name, distance). |
| **Cursor change** | Improves discoverability that the lines are clickable. |

---

## 3️⃣ When (and how) to switch to vector tiles later

If you ever notice:

* **File size creeping above ~5 MB** (e.g., you add elevation profiles, more attributes, or higher-precision coordinates), **or**
* **Parsing lag > 2 s on typical mobile devices**,  

then moving the trails to vector tiles will give you:

* **Viewport-only loading** (only the tiles covering the current view are fetched).
* **Zoom-dependent simplification** (you can supply different detail levels per zoom).
* **Smaller network payloads** (tippecanoe compresses geometry heavily).

### Quick “future-proof” workflow

1. **Generate MBTiles / PMTiles** with `tippecanoe`:

   ```bash
   tippecanoe \
     --output=trails.pmtiles \
     --layer=trails \
     --minimum-zoom=5 \
     --maximum-zoom=14 \
     --drop-densest-as-needed \
     --simplify-only-low-zoom \
     trails.geojson
   ```

   *`--drop-densest-as-needed`* automatically removes the most detailed points when the tile would exceed the size limit, keeping the tiles under ~50 KB each.

2. **Serve the tiles** – you can use a static file server (PMTiles is just a single file) together with the **MapLibre PMTiles plugin** (`maplibre-pmtiles-patterns` skill) or a lightweight tile server like **tileserver-gl**.

3. **Add the vector source** (instead of the GeoJSON source) in the same style:

   ```js
   map.addSource('trails', {
     type: 'vector',
     url: 'pmtiles://trails.pmtiles'   // or a TileJSON URL if you host via tileserver-gl
   });
   map.addLayer({
     id: 'trails-line',
     type: 'line',
     source: 'trails',
     'source-layer': 'trails',   // name you gave in tippecanoe
     paint: { ... }
   });
   ```

4. **Keep the two-source pattern** – basemap stays separate, your trail source stays separate. This lets you upgrade the basemap or swap out the trail tiles without rebuilding the whole world.

---

## 4️⃣ Extra performance & UX tips for the GeoJSON approach

| Tip | How to apply |
|-----|--------------|
| **Simplify geometries** (if they are very dense) | Use a tool like **`geojson-reduce`**, **`mapshaper`**, or **`ogr2ogr -simplify`** to drop points that are < 1 m apart. This can cut file size dramatically without visible loss at typical web zoom levels. |
| **Reduce coordinate precision** | Round coordinates to 5-6 decimal places (`~0.1 m` precision) before publishing. |
| **Compress the file** | Serve the GeoJSON with **gzip** or **brotli** (`Content-Encoding: gzip`). Browsers will decompress automatically, often cutting the transfer size by 70-80 %. |
| **Use `generateId: true`** (optional) | Gives each feature a stable numeric id, which speeds up `queryRenderedFeatures` and click handling. |
| **Cluster (if you ever need points)** | Not needed for line trails, but if you later add trailheads as points, you can enable `cluster: true` on the GeoJSON source. |
| **Lazy-load for very large regions** | If you have a national dataset but only show a small area, consider splitting the GeoJSON into regional files and loading the appropriate one on `moveend`. This is overkill for 8 k features but useful to know. |

---

## 5️⃣ TL;DR checklist

- ✅ **Use a `geojson` source** for the 8 000 trail segments.  
- ✅ **Use a hosted vector-tile basemap** (terrain + roads).  
- ✅ Insert the trail layer **before the first symbol layer** so labels stay on top.  
- ✅ Add a click handler that reads `properties.name` and `properties.distance`.  
- ✅ Keep an eye on file size; if it grows > 5 MB or parsing > 2 s, switch the trails to **vector tiles** with `tippecanoe` (or Planetiler) and keep the two-source pattern.  

That’s all you need to get a fast, interactive hiking-trail map with a beautiful terrain/road basemap in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!

**Short answer:** No – 50 points is far below the size where a vector-tile server becomes worthwhile. A simple **GeoJSON source** (inline or fetched from a static file) will load instantly, let you query each store for pop-ups, and requires no tile infrastructure at all.

