## Putting it together with a basemap

Most store-locator apps also need a background map (roads, labels, etc.). The cleanest pattern is:

```javascript
// 1️⃣ Basemap – use a hosted vector style URL (e.g., MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, or your own style)
map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY',
  center: [-98.5795, 39.8283], // continental US
  zoom: 4
});

// 2️⃣ Add your GeoJSON source & layers (as shown above)
//   – insert your layers *before* the first symbol layer if you want them under labels
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // optional: find first symbol layer to insert before
  const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;
  map.addSource('stores', { type: 'geojson', data: '/stores.geojson' });
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'store-points',
    type: 'circle',
    source: 'stores',
    paint: { 'circle-color': '#ff6600', 'circle-radius': 6, 'circle-stroke-width': 1, 'circle-stroke-color': '#fff' }
  }, firstSymbolId); // insert before labels
});
```

This **two-source pattern** (basemap + your GeoJSON) is the standard, most maintainable approach. It avoids the unnecessary cost of merging your stores into the basemap tileset, and you can swap the basemap style or provider at any time without rebuilding anything.

---

### TL;DR Checklist for a 50-store locator

1. **Create a GeoJSON file** (`stores.geojson`) with the 50 point features.  
2. **Add a `geojson` source** to your MapLibre map.  
3. **Add a point layer** (circle or symbol) that references that source.  
4. **Add click/hover pop-ups** for store details.  
5. **Optionally cluster** if you want a cleaner view at low zoom.  
6. **Use any vector-tile basemap style** (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.) as the background; keep your store data as a separate source.

No vector-tile server, no tile generation, no extra hosting costs – just a static GeoJSON file and a few lines of MapLibre configuration. Happy mapping!

**Use a **`vector`** tile source for the basemap.**  

Vector tiles contain only the raw geometry + attributes; the visual appearance (colors, line-weights, label fonts, etc.) is defined entirely in the **style** that you give to MapLibre. Because the style lives on the client, you can swap between a light-theme style and a dark-theme style at runtime **without ever touching the tile data**.  

---

## Why raster tiles won’t work for this use-case

| Tile type | Where the colour/label decisions are made | What you need to change for a theme switch |
|-----------|-------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
| **raster** (`raster` source) | In the pre-rendered PNG/JPEG tiles themselves | You would have to host two complete tile sets (light + dark) and swap the source URL, which defeats the “single-set” requirement. |
| **vector** (`vector` source) | In the client-side style JSON (or programmatic style changes) | One tile set serves both themes; you only change the style object (or toggle layer paint properties). |

Hence, the only tile type that lets you keep **one** tile set and still have two completely different visual appearances is **vector tiles**.

---

## How to implement a light/dark switch with a single vector source

### 1. Host one vector tile set (e.g. PMTiles, TileJSON, XYZ, or a hosted service)

```json
// tile source definition (shared by both themes)
{
  "type": "vector",
  "url": "https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
}
```

### 2. Create two *style* documents that reference the same source

*light-theme style (`style-light.json`)*  

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "basemap": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    // background, land, water, roads, labels … all with light colours
    { "id": "background", "type": "background", "paint": { "background-color": "#f8f9fa" } },
    { "id": "water", "type": "fill", "source": "basemap", "source-layer": "water", "paint": { "fill-color": "#cce6ff" } },
    …
  ],
  "glyphs": "https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "sprite": "https://example.com/sprites/light"
}
```

*dark-theme style (`style-dark.json`)* – same `sources` block, but different paint values, background colour, and optionally a different sprite sheet.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "basemap": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    { "id": "background", "type": "background", "paint": { "background-color": "#111111" } },
    { "id": "water", "type": "fill", "source": "basemap", "source-layer": "water", "paint": { "fill-color": "#2b3d5e" } },
    …
  ],
  "glyphs": "https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "sprite": "https://example.com/sprites/dark"
}
```

> **Tip:** If you only need a few colour swaps you can keep a single style and toggle `paint` properties at runtime instead of loading two separate JSON files. See the “Dynamic style switching” section below.

### 3. Load the map and let the user toggle

```javascript
// initial load – start with the light theme
let map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://example.com/style-light.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// UI button to toggle
document.getElementById('themeToggle').addEventListener('click', () => {
  const newStyle = map.getStyle().metadata?.theme === 'dark'
    ? 'https://example.com/style-light.json'
    : 'https://example.com/style-dark.json';

  // Preserve the current view (center, zoom, bearing, pitch)
  const { lng, lat } = map.getCenter();
  const zoom = map.getZoom();
  const bearing = map.getBearing();
  const pitch = map.getPitch();

  map.setStyle(newStyle);

  // When the new style finishes loading, restore the view
  map.once('styledata', () => {
    map.jumpTo({ center: [lng, lat], zoom, bearing, pitch });
    // optional: keep a flag in style metadata so we know which theme is active
    map.getStyle().metadata = { theme: newStyle.includes('dark') ? 'dark' : 'light' };
  });
});
```

### 4. (Optional) **Dynamic paint-property swapping** – no extra style files

If you prefer a single style JSON and only need to invert colours, you can keep the same style and change paint values on the fly:

```javascript
function setDarkTheme(isDark) {
  const bg = isDark ? '#111' : '#f8f9fa';
  const water = isDark ? '#2b3d5e' : '#cce6ff';
  // background
  map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', bg);
  // water layer (assuming its id is "water")
  map.setPaintProperty('water', 'fill-color', water);
  // repeat for roads, land, labels, etc.
}

// UI toggle
let dark = false;
document.getElementById('themeToggle').addEventListener('click', () => {
  dark = !dark;
  setDarkTheme(dark);
});
```

*Pros:* No extra network request for a second style file.  
*Cons:* You have to manually keep track of every paint property you want to invert; a full style file is easier to maintain and version-control.

---

## Summary checklist

| ✅ | Decision |
|---|----------|
| **Tile type** | `vector` (single tile set) |
| **Theme switch method** | Load a second style (`map.setStyle`) **or** toggle paint properties (`map.setPaintProperty`) |
| **What stays the same** | Tile URL / source definition, glyphs, sprite (you can keep separate sprite sheets for light/dark if you need different icons) |
| **What changes** | `background-color`, layer `paint` values, possibly `sprite` URL |
| **No extra tile hosting** | Because the visual appearance is decoupled from the tile data, you only host the vector tiles once. |

---

### Quick “copy-paste” starter

```javascript
// 1️⃣ Create a single vector source in both style files (or in a shared style fragment)
{
  "basemap": {
    "type": "vector",
    "url": "https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
  }
}

// 2️⃣ Two style URLs (light & dark) that reference the same source
const LIGHT_STYLE = 'https://example.com/style-light.json';
const DARK_STYLE  = 'https://example.com/style-dark.json';

// 3️⃣ Map init
let map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: LIGHT_STYLE,
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],
  zoom: 12
});

// 4️⃣ Theme toggle
let isDark = false;
document.getElementById('themeToggle').onclick = () => {
  isDark = !isDark;
  const nextStyle = isDark ? DARK_STYLE : LIGHT_STYLE;
  const view = map.getFreeCameraOptions(); // preserves center/zoom/etc.
  map.setStyle(nextStyle);
  map.once('styledata', () => map.setFreeCameraOptions(view));
};
```

That’s all you need: **one vector tile set, two style definitions (or dynamic paint changes), and a runtime `setStyle` call**. This gives you a light/dark basemap that can be toggled instantly without duplicating any tile data.

**Short answer:** Yes. You can keep your Leaflet app and embed a MapLibre GL JS map (which can consume vector-tile basemaps) as a Leaflet layer. The easiest way is to use the **MapLibre GL Leaflet** plugin.  

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus the two other options you might consider (full migration to MapLibre GL JS or using a raster-tile proxy).

---

## 1️⃣ What the plugin does

| Feature | Leaflet + MapLibre GL Leaflet |
|---------|------------------------------|
| **Vector-tile basemap** | ✅ MapLibre GL JS renders the vector tiles client-side (styles can be changed on the fly). |
| **Leaflet UI & controls** | ✅ All existing Leaflet controls (zoom bar, layer control, popups, etc.) keep working because the MapLibre map is wrapped as a normal Leaflet `Layer`. |
| **Interaction with Leaflet layers** | ✅ You can still add Leaflet `Marker`, `Circle`, `GeoJSON`, etc. on top of the MapLibre basemap. |
| **Performance** | ✅ Only one extra canvas is added; the MapLibre renderer is hardware-accelerated. |
| **Limitations** | – The MapLibre map is a *single* layer; you cannot mix a second MapLibre source inside the same canvas without rebuilding the style. <br>– Some MapLibre-only APIs (e.g., `map.queryRenderedFeatures`) are only reachable through the underlying `maplibregl.Map` instance that the plugin exposes. |

---

## 2️⃣ Install the plugin (and MapLibre GL JS)

```bash
# Using npm / yarn
npm install maplibre-gl maplibre-gl-leaflet
# or with a CDN (simpler for quick demos)
```

**CDN example (HTML):**

```html
<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
/>
<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
/>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Version tip:** Use the latest stable versions of both libraries. The plugin tracks MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.0, so any recent release works.

---

## 3️⃣ Create a Leaflet map and add the MapLibre layer

```javascript
// 1️⃣ Initialise the Leaflet map as usual
const leafletMap = L.map('map', {
  center: [40.7128, -74.0060], // New York City
  zoom: 12,
  // optional: keep the default Leaflet CRS (EPSG:3857) – MapLibre uses the same.
});

// 2️⃣ Add a regular Leaflet base layer (optional, for fallback)
L.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
  attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors',
}).addTo(leafletMap);

// 3️⃣ Add the MapLibre GL layer
const mapLibreLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  // ----> REQUIRED: a MapLibre style that points to vector tiles
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // public demo style
  // optional: you can also pass a style object instead of a URL
  // style: { version: 8, sources: {...}, layers: [...] }

  // ----> OPTIONAL: expose the underlying MapLibre GL map for later use
  // (e.g., to call map.queryRenderedFeatures)
  // you can later get it via: const mlMap = mapLibreLayer.getMap();
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

### What the code does

1. **Leaflet map** – Handles all UI, controls, and any Leaflet-only layers you already have.
2. **`L.maplibreGL`** – Creates a *single* Leaflet `Layer` that internally creates a `maplibregl.Map` instance, points it at the supplied style, and syncs the view (center, zoom, bearing, pitch) with the Leaflet map on every interaction.
3. The style you provide can be any **vector-tile basemap** you control or a public one (MapLibre’s demo style, MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.). The style JSON must contain a `vector` source (or a `raster` source if you prefer raster tiles).

---

## 4️⃣ Adding your own data on top (Leaflet side)

Because the MapLibre basemap lives inside a Leaflet layer, you can keep using Leaflet’s `L.geoJSON`, `L.marker`, `L.circle`, etc.:

```javascript
// Example: a small GeoJSON overlay (still < 2 MB → fine as GeoJSON)
const myPoints = L.geoJSON(myGeojson, {
  pointToLayer: (feature, latlng) =>
    L.circleMarker(latlng, { radius: 6, color: '#ff6600' })
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

If you later need **vector-tile** overlays (e.g., a large dataset), you can add a *second* MapLibre GL layer with its own style, or you can merge the overlay into the same style JSON (see “Two-source pattern” below). The plugin lets you stack multiple `L.maplibreGL` layers, each with its own style.

---

## 5️⃣ Accessing the underlying MapLibre map (advanced)

Sometimes you need MapLibre-only APIs (e.g., `queryRenderedFeatures`, `setPaintProperty`). The plugin exposes the internal map:

```javascript
// After the layer has been added and the style is loaded:
mapLibreLayer.on('load', () => {
  const mlMap = mapLibreLayer.getMap(); // <-- maplibregl.Map instance

  // Example: query features under the mouse cursor
  leafletMap.on('click', (e) => {
    const features = mlMap.queryRenderedFeatures(
      mlMap.project([e.latlng.lng, e.latlng.lat]), // convert to pixel coords
      { layers: ['road', 'water'] }               // optional filter
    );
    console.log('MapLibre features at click:', features);
  });
});
```

> **Note:** The coordinate conversion (`mlMap.project`) is necessary because MapLibre expects Web Mercator pixel coordinates, while Leaflet gives you lat/lng.

---

## 6️⃣ Two-source pattern (recommended for most apps)

Even though you can technically bundle your own data into the same vector-tile source as the basemap, the **standard, low-maintenance approach** is:

1. **Basemap source** – a hosted vector-tile style (e.g., MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, your own TileServer).  
2. **Overlay source** – either a Leaflet `GeoJSON` layer (small data) **or** a second MapLibre GL layer that points to a separate vector-tile source you generate (large data).

Why this pattern?

| Reason | Explanation |
|--------|--------------|
| **No rebuild of the basemap** | Updating your data never forces you to re-tile the whole world. |
| **Licensing & attribution** | You keep the basemap’s attribution separate from your data’s. |
| **Styling flexibility** | You can change the basemap style (e.g., switch from light to dark) without touching your overlay tiles. |
| **Performance** | MapLibre GL JS requests tiles from each source concurrently; there is no per-source penalty. |

If you ever need a *single* source (offline kiosk, demo with zero network), you can still generate a combined tileset with **tippecanoe** or **Planetiler**, but the above two-source pattern is the default for production web apps.

---

## 7️⃣ Alternative: Full migration to MapLibre GL JS

If you find yourself needing many vector-tile features (e.g., heavy interactivity, 3-D terrain, custom shaders), you might consider **dropping Leaflet entirely** and building the map with MapLibre GL JS directly. The migration steps are:

1. Replace `L.map` with `new maplibregl.Map({ container: 'map', style: <your-style>, center, zoom })`.
2. Port any Leaflet controls you need to MapLibre equivalents (or reuse Leaflet controls via the `maplibre-gl-leaflet` plugin’s `L.Control` wrappers).
3. Add your GeoJSON data with a `geojson` source or a `vector` source, as shown in the **maplibre-source-wiring** skill.

The official **Leaflet migration guide** walks you through the details: <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/guides/leaflet-migration-guide/>

---

## 8️⃣ Quick checklist before you ship

| ✅ Checklist | Details |
|--------------|---------|
| **MapLibre GL JS version** | Use the latest stable (≥ 4.0 at time of writing). |
| **Style URL** | Points to a valid vector-tile style (check with the MapLibre style validator). |
| **CORS** | Vector-tile endpoints must allow cross-origin requests from your domain (or use a proxy). |
| **Attribution** | Add the basemap’s attribution to your Leaflet control (`L.control.attribution`). |
| **Mobile performance** | Test on a low-end device; vector tiles are usually < 50 KB per tile at 512 px, but many concurrent requests can still be heavy. |
| **Fallback** | Keep a raster OSM tile layer as a fallback for browsers that cannot run WebGL (rare, but easy to add). |
| **License compliance** | Verify that the basemap’s license permits your use case (e.g., CC-BY-SA, ODbL). |
| **Debugging** | Open the browser console; look for `maplibre-gl` warnings about missing sprites, glyphs, or source-layer names. |

---

## 9️⃣ Minimal working example (HTML)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre GL vector basemap</title>
  <style>
    #map { position: absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:0; right:0; }
  </style>

  <!-- Leaflet -->
  <link rel="stylesheet"
        href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css" />
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

  <!-- MapLibre GL -->
  <link rel="stylesheet"
        href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" />
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <!-- Plugin -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // Leaflet map
  const map = L.map('map').setView([40.7128, -74.0060], 12);

  // Optional Leaflet fallback base layer (visible while MapLibre loads)
  L.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
    attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
  }).addTo(map);

  // MapLibre GL vector basemap
  const mlLayer = L.maplibreGL({
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    // you can also pass `attribution: '…'` here if you want it in Leaflet's control
  }).addTo(map);

  // Example Leaflet overlay (small GeoJSON)
  const geojson = {
    "type": "FeatureCollection",
    "features": [
      { "type":"Feature","properties":{},"geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-74.006,40.7128]} }
    ]
  };
  L.geoJSON(geojson, {
    pointToLayer: (f, latlng) => L.circleMarker(latlng, {radius:8, fillColor:'#ff6600', color:'#fff', weight:1, opacity:1, fillOpacity:0.9})
  }).addTo(map);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser and you’ll see a fully functional Leaflet UI with a crisp vector-tile basemap rendered by MapLibre GL JS.

---

## TL;DR

| Goal | Recommended approach |
|------|----------------------|
| **Add a vector-tile basemap to an existing Leaflet app** | Use **MapLibre GL Leaflet** (`L.maplibreGL`) – one line of code, no need to rewrite the whole app. |
| **Need heavy vector-tile interactivity or multiple vector sources** | Keep the plugin, but add additional `L.maplibreGL` layers or migrate to pure MapLibre GL JS. |
| **Offline / single-file deployment** | Build a combined vector-tile PMTiles/MBTiles archive (tippecanoe/Planetiler) and load it with `maplibregl.Map` directly – still possible inside Leaflet via the plugin, but usually you’d just use MapLibre alone. |
